          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why do some categories need a fourth or fifth character while others do not?",
          "answer": "In the US clinical modification of ICD-10 (ICD-10-CM), a three-character code is a category. Some categories stop there, and others add characters to narrow the meaning. The source material I retrieved says that characters four and five add detail such as cause, anatomical site, or severity. Character six often records laterality, meaning right, left, or unspecified. Some categories also use a seventh character, which describes the episode of care rather than the condition. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\nThe classification adds these subdivisions because the US version is much more detailed than the WHO version it extends. It keeps the WHO chapters and most three-character categories, then subdivides them further. (Source: *ICD-10-CM and WHO ICD-10*.)\n\nHypertension is an example of a category that needs no extra characters. I10 is complete at three characters, while most categories need a fourth, fifth, or sixth character before they count as billable. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10*.)\n\nOther categories are built along axes. Asthma (J45) is never used at three characters. Its severity axis and its exacerbation axis are combined, so every pairing has its own code. (Source: *Coding asthma: J45*.) Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (J44) has no severity axis. Its fourth character records whether an infection or an exacerbation is present, and a stable presentation uses the unspecified code. (Source: *Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44*.)\n\nI could not find in these passages a complete rule that lists which categories require which character counts. Those rules sit in the official ICD-10-CM tabular list and its coding guidelines, which I did not retrieve. For an actual code assignment, check those official sources or ask a trained medical coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
